"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunk_britecharts_docs||=[]).push([[2241],{1995(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"britecharts-3-beta-8","metadata":{"permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-8","editUrl":"https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/edit/main/packages/docs/blog/blog/2026-10-29-britecharts-3-beta-8/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-29-britecharts-3-beta-8/index.md","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 8, accessible by default","description":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.8 is out! Every chart in it can be read by a screen reader cell by cell, used with a keyboard, themed with CSS and drawn in dark mode, with no setup. It is also the first version built for coding agents as well as for people.","date":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Releases","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/tags/releases","description":"Britecharts releases and what is in them"}],"readingTime":6.2,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Marcos Iglesias Valle","title":"Maintainer of Britecharts","url":"https://github.com/golodhros","imageURL":"https://github.com/golodhros.png","key":"marcos","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"britecharts-3-beta-8","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 8, accessible by default","authors":["marcos"],"tags":["releases"]},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 7, the last beta","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-7"}},"content":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.8 is out! Every chart in it can be read by a screen reader cell by cell, used with a keyboard, themed with CSS and drawn in dark mode, with no setup. It is also the first version built for coding agents as well as for people.\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install @britecharts/core@beta\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Why there is another beta\\n\\nI wrote that beta 7 would be the last beta, and that only bug fixes would come before 3.0.0. That did not happen. I compared Britecharts with eight current charting libraries, and the comparison showed what Britecharts can do better than any of them: **finished charts that are accessible by default, free, and easy for an agent to write**. Most of that work changes the API, so it had to land before 3.0.0 fixes the API in place.\\n\\n## Accessible by default\\n\\nOnly one library in that comparison gave readers a data table, keyboard navigation and reduced motion together, and it charges per seat. Every Britecharts chart now does all of it, with each part on by default and each one possible to turn off. See [how to make a chart accessible](/docs/how-tos/user-how-to-guides#how-to-make-a-chart-accessible).\\n\\n- **Its data as a table.** Every chart but the legend renders its data as a visually hidden table after its svg, so a screen reader can read it cell by cell. Series charts lay it out with a row per date or name and a column per series. Turn it off with `a11yDataTable(false)`.\\n- **A keyboard can move through the data.** The bar, donut, grouped bar, heatmap, line, scatter plot, stacked area and stacked bar charts are each one tab stop. The arrow keys move between points, Home and End go to the ends, and Escape lets go. The point is highlighted and the tooltip follows focus, as they do for a pointer. Turn it off with `a11yKeyboardNavigation(false)`.\\n- **Still when the reader asks.** With `prefers-reduced-motion`, the charts draw without animation whatever `isAnimated` says, and the tooltip and legend stop fading.\\n- **A colour-blind-safe palette.** `colors.colorSchemas.okabeIto` keeps its eight colours apart with the common kinds of colour blindness.\\n\\n## Themes and dark mode, with CSS\\n\\nEvery colour around the data is now a `--bc-*` custom property: text, axes, grid, tooltips, legend and loading states. The charts follow your page\'s `color-scheme` through `light-dark()`, so `color-scheme: light dark` on your page is all dark mode needs. To change one colour, set its token, such as `.britechart { --bc-text-muted: #555 }`. A page that declares no colour scheme stays light. See [how to theme a chart](/docs/how-tos/user-how-to-guides#how-to-theme-a-chart-and-support-dark-mode).\\n\\n## Made for coding agents\\n\\nAn agent writes a chart correctly more often when the library tells it what it needs. This beta adds four ways to do that:\\n\\n- **An agent skill** ships in `@britecharts/core`, with each chart\'s data shape, accessors, events and the v2 code that no longer works. Install it with `npx skills add britecharts/britecharts`.\\n- **A JSON Schema for every chart\'s spec**, `{ chart, config, data }`, generated from the TypeScript types and published at `https://britecharts.github.io/britecharts/schema/v3/<chart>.json`. An editor or an agent can check a spec before drawing it. See [how to describe a chart as a spec](/docs/how-tos/user-how-to-guides#how-to-describe-a-chart-as-a-spec-and-check-it).\\n- **Errors that say how to fix them.** A misspelt key gets `heigth: did you mean height?` and a link to the chart\'s API page. A value of the wrong type names the type it should be.\\n- **[llms.txt](pathname:///llms.txt)** describes every chart and accessor, generated from the same comments as the API pages.\\n\\n## New in the charts\\n\\n- **100% stacks.** `isNormalized(true)` stretches every stack of the stacked bar, or every date of the stacked area, to fill the value axis, which then reads from 0% to 100%.\\n- **Any categories in the heatmap.** `xAxisLabels` joins `yAxisLabels`, so a heatmap can compare any two sets of categories, such as a square grid of six.\\n- **SVG export.** `exportChart(\'chart.svg\')` downloads an svg with its styles inlined, so it looks the same outside the page and stays sharp at any size.\\n- **Every chart in every package.** The brush and the heatmap now have a wrapper and a React component, like the other charts.\\n- **Fixes you may have hit.** The brush no longer flickers on a second selection or fires `customBrushEnd` while idle. The animated stacked bar grows each stack as one block. The legend\'s highlight commands no longer throw before it draws. A bullet can have more than one marker.\\n- **Smaller app bundles.** Each package declares `sideEffects`, so bundlers can drop the charts an app does not import. In a bar-only test app, the bundle went from 104.0 kB to 89.6 kB.\\n\\n![A square heatmap in purple that compares six light words, Blazing, Glittering, Shining, Sunny, Flashing and Radiant, with each other. The diagonal is darkest, where each word appears on its own.](./heatmap-categories.png)\\n\\nThe [Storybook](pathname:///storybook/) shows all of it, with the same stories under the same names in core and in React.\\n\\n## What to check before you upgrade\\n\\nFive changes in this beta can affect a page that works with beta 7:\\n\\n1. **Charts add a tab stop and a hidden table.** If your page has its own table of the same data, or a tab order to keep, turn them off with `a11yDataTable(false)` or `a11yKeyboardNavigation(false)`.\\n2. **The wrappers check value types.** `create` and `update` throw on a value of the wrong type, such as `height: \\"300\\"`. Before, the string reached the chart.\\n3. **An exported title gets its own band.** The title sits in a 30px band above the chart instead of over its top edge, so an exported image with a title is 30px taller.\\n4. **The heatmap\'s label classes changed.** `.day-label` is now `.row-label`, `.hour-label` is `.column-label`, and their groups are `.row-labels-group` and `.column-labels-group`. The row labels now end at the grid, and both axes share one label style.\\n5. **Some colours are set as styles now.** The tooltip\'s text, the donut\'s empty slice, the scatter plot\'s hollow circles, the line chart\'s custom lines, the sparkline\'s gradient and the heatmap\'s labels take their colours from the `--bc-*` tokens, set on the element. A CSS rule on those elements no longer wins, so set the token instead.\\n\\nIf you are coming from version 2, the [migration guide](/docs/how-tos/migration-guide-2-to-3) has the full list.\\n\\n## What happens next\\n\\nThe API work I wanted before 3.0.0 is done, so this time I mean it: what follows is bug fixes, then 3.0.0. Bigger features, such as server-side rendering, a Canvas renderer or Web Component bindings, wait as [issues that need votes](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues?q=label%3Astatus%3Aneeds_votes). Add a \ud83d\udc4d to the ones you would use.\\n\\nPlease try this beta in a real project, with a screen reader and a keyboard if you can. Open an [issue](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues) with the chart, the data and what you expected.\\n\\nBritecharts still needs maintainers. If you would like to own part of it, the [contributing guide](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md) is where to start."},{"id":"britecharts-3-beta-7","metadata":{"permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-7","editUrl":"https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/edit/main/packages/docs/blog/blog/2026-10-18-britecharts-3-beta-7/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-18-britecharts-3-beta-7/index.md","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 7, the last beta","description":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.7 is out, and it is the last beta. The next release I plan to publish is 3.0.0 itself. Test against this version if you want your bug fixed before the stable release rather than after it.","date":"2026-10-18T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Releases","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/tags/releases","description":"Britecharts releases and what is in them"}],"readingTime":4.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Marcos Iglesias Valle","title":"Maintainer of Britecharts","url":"https://github.com/golodhros","imageURL":"https://github.com/golodhros.png","key":"marcos","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"britecharts-3-beta-7","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 7, the last beta","authors":["marcos"],"tags":["releases"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 8, accessible by default","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-8"},"nextItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta"}},"content":"Britecharts 3.0.0-beta.7 is out, and it is the last beta. The next release I plan to publish is 3.0.0 itself. Test against this version if you want your bug fixed before the stable release rather than after it.\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install @britecharts/core@beta\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed since the first beta\\n\\nWhen I announced the beta in September, I said it held more than a version bump. The betas since then have made the packages behave the way modern tooling expects. They also fixed the places where the charts said one thing and did another. Here is what you will notice:\\n\\n- **One bundle per package, built with Vite.** Version 2 published a file for every chart. Version 3 now publishes one UMD bundle and one CDN bundle per package, plus ES modules for bundlers. Bundlers already drop the charts you do not import, so a file per chart saved nothing there. On a page with a script tag, one request is better than five.\\n- **Real ES module packages.** The three packages declare `\\"type\\": \\"module\\"`, so `import` works in Node as well as in bundlers, with matching types for each way of loading. `require` still works and loads the CommonJS bundle, which now ends in `.cjs`.\\n- **Charts that screen readers can name.** Every chart gives its svg a role and an accessible name, \\"Bar chart\\" by default. Set `a11yTitle` to name it after what it shows. Set `a11yDescription` to add a sentence about what it means.\\n- **Negative values in the scatter plot.** Its value axis now runs below zero when the data does, and each circle\'s size shows the size of its value. That completes the negative values work: the bar, grouped bar, stacked bar, brush and scatter plot charts all draw them.\\n- **Typings that match the charts.** I compared every chart\'s published types with what it does at run time, and fixed more than a hundred mismatches. Getters were declared as setters, callbacks had the wrong return type, and accessors were declared but never existed. If TypeScript accepts a call now, the chart does what the call says.\\n- **Smaller fixes you may have hit.** The brush honours `locale` and the stacked area honours `numberFormat`, which both used to do nothing. The scatter plot\'s zoom works again. The grouped and stacked bar tooltips no longer flicker when you move across the bars. Resting the pointer on a chart no longer pops up the browser\'s own tooltip. A chart\'s `valueLocale` no longer changes the numbers of every other chart on the page. A `colorMap` that names only some categories colours the rest from the palette.\\n- **Tooltips that say what each chart needs.** The charts name the fields of the data they send the tooltip differently. Some of them need the tooltip told where to look: `topicLabel(\'values\')` for the stacked area, `nameLabel(\'stack\')` or `nameLabel(\'group\')` for the stacked and grouped bars, `valueLabel(\'y\')` for the scatter plot. The [tooltip reference](/docs/API/tooltip) and a new [how-to](/docs/how-tos/user-how-to-guides) list them per chart. Every story shows them in use.\\n- **Leaner API pages and more recipes.** Each accessor\'s heading starts with its name instead of `exports.`, in the pages and in their tables of contents. The [how-to guides](/docs/how-tos/user-how-to-guides) gain recipes for per-category colours, number and date formats, loading states, animation, negative values and accessibility.\\n- **In React,** `Tooltip` infers its data type from its `data` prop, so its `render` callback type-checks without a cast. Seven settings the charts always had are now props, and `prop-types` is no longer a peer dependency.\\n\\n## What to check before you upgrade\\n\\nIf you tried an earlier beta, five changes in this stretch can break your code. The [migration guide](/docs/how-tos/migration-guide-2-to-3) covers each of them with a before and after:\\n\\n1. **Per-chart files are gone.** Import the charts from the package, or load `dist/cdn/bundle/core.cdn.min.js` and use the `core` global.\\n2. **CommonJS bundles end in `.cjs`.** This matters only if you required a bundle by its path.\\n3. **The bullet chart and the sparkline no longer draw titles.** Text inside an svg cannot wrap and ignores your page\'s typography, so put the label in your own markup and pass the same text to `a11yTitle`.\\n4. **An accessor you have not set reads `null`,** on every chart. Before, some charts returned `undefined`. A truthiness check works as before.\\n5. **The data-key accessors are gone.** `nameLabel`, `valueLabel`, `dateLabel` and the rest no longer let a chart read your data under other keys. They had warned since version 2 that they would go. Rename the keys in your data to the ones the chart\'s data shape names, such as `name` and `value`. The tooltip keeps its own four.\\n\\nIf you are coming from version 2, the guide has the full list. Version 2 ends at 2.18.2.\\n\\n## What happens next\\n\\nBetween now and 3.0.0, I only plan to fix bugs. The API is settled, so if something in it is wrong, now is the last cheap moment to say so. After 3.0.0 ships, changing it means another major version.\\n\\nSo please try this beta in a real project. Open an [issue](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues) with the chart, the data and what you expected. The [Storybook](pathname:///storybook/) shows every chart and its options, in core and in React. It is the fastest way to check whether something is a bug or a setting.\\n\\nBritecharts still needs maintainers. If you would like to own part of it, the [contributing guide](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md) is where to start."},{"id":"britecharts-3-beta","metadata":{"permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta","editUrl":"https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/edit/main/packages/docs/blog/blog/2026-09-13-britecharts-3-beta/index.md","source":"@site/blog/2026-09-13-britecharts-3-beta/index.md","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","description":"Britecharts 3.0 is in beta. I split it into three packages this time \u2014 @britecharts/core for the charts themselves, @britecharts/react for the React components, and @britecharts/wrappers, the layer the React components are built on and, I hope, the seam a Vue or Svelte binding will eventually grow out of.","date":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Releases","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/tags/releases","description":"Britecharts releases and what is in them"}],"readingTime":4.22,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Marcos Iglesias Valle","title":"Maintainer of Britecharts","url":"https://github.com/golodhros","imageURL":"https://github.com/golodhros.png","key":"marcos","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"britecharts-3-beta","title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta","authors":["marcos"],"tags":["releases"]},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Britecharts 3.0 beta 7, the last beta","permalink":"/britecharts/blog/britecharts-3-beta-7"}},"content":"Britecharts 3.0 is in beta. I split it into three packages this time \u2014 `@britecharts/core` for the charts themselves, `@britecharts/react` for the React components, and `@britecharts/wrappers`, the layer the React components are built on and, I hope, the seam a Vue or Svelte binding will eventually grow out of.\\n\\n```sh\\nnpm install @britecharts/core@beta\\n```\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What is new\\n\\nThere is more here than a version bump, so let me go through it in the order I think you will actually notice it:\\n\\n- **Modern modules.** Built on the current d3 modules, shipped as ES modules, CommonJS, UMD and CDN builds, with an `exports` map and TypeScript typings for every chart. Vite, Rollup, esbuild and webpack all work out of the box, which was not true of v2 \u2014 every bundler had its own workaround, and I was tired of maintaining them.\\n- **Smaller bundles.** The build now targets ES2020, and Babel is gone entirely from `@britecharts/core` and `@britecharts/wrappers` \u2014 the core UMD bundle drops 12.7% (151,923 \u2192 132,626 bytes) and the React bundle 14% (368 \u2192 315 KB). You do not need to do anything for this one; a browser below the new target already needs a bundler or its own polyfills to run modern syntax at all, so nothing that worked before stops working now.\\n- **One tooltip.** `tooltip` and `miniTooltip` used to be two components with two sets of bugs. They are one now, and it never gets cut off at the chart\'s edges, follows the pointer smoothly, and behaves the same way whichever chart it is attached to.\\n- **Negative values** on the bar, stacked bar, grouped bar and brush charts, stacking down from zero. This has been the second-most-requested thing since 2019, and the reason it took this long is that I kept underestimating how much of the domain math assumed zero was the floor.\\n- **Loading states** for every chart, `colorMap` to name the colour of each category, `animationDuration` on every animated chart, and charts that are responsive by default.\\n- **React 19** is tested end to end, not just claimed: the unit suite itself now runs on React 19, and the React package is also installed into a real React 19 project under `StrictMode` on every change. If you have been holding off upgrading because you weren\'t sure the wrappers would survive `StrictMode`\'s double-invoked effects, that is exactly what this checks.\\n\\n## Moving from version 2\\n\\nMost of the work is renaming, and I tried to keep it that way on purpose \u2014 a rewrite nobody can follow is worse than a major version nobody wants to install. The [migration guide](/docs/how-tos/migration-guide-2-to-3) walks through every breaking change with a before and after for each. The ones you will hit first:\\n\\n1. **Imports.** The charts are named exports of `@britecharts/core` (`import { bar } from \'@britecharts/core\'`) instead of files under `britecharts/dist/umd`. The CommonJS, UMD and CDN paths are in the guide too.\\n2. **Loading states.** `loadingState()` is gone; render the chart with `isLoading(true)` and a dataset, empty or not.\\n3. **Renamed accessors.** `locale` is `valueLocale` on the bar, grouped bar, stacked bar and scatter plot; the value formatters of the grouped and stacked bar are `numberFormat`; the line and stacked area swap `xAxisFormat` and `xAxisCustomFormat` back to what their names say; `aspectRatio` is removed.\\n4. **Tooltips.** The line, stacked area, stacked bar and grouped bar charts now dispatch `customMouseMove` the same way every other chart does, as `(dataPoint, [x, y], [width, height], colorMap)`. If you drive `tooltip.update` yourself, it still accepts the old argument order and warns once.\\n5. **`exportChart`** returns a promise.\\n\\nVersion 2 ends at 2.18.2. I am not going to pretend I will keep patching it once 3.0 ships, so if you are on v2 and this list looks manageable, better to do it now than after a security report forces the question.\\n\\n## Where to start\\n\\n- The [installation](/docs/tutorials/installing-britecharts) and [getting started](/docs/tutorials/getting-started) tutorials build a first chart from scratch, and [styling charts](/docs/tutorials/styling-charts) covers themes and CSS.\\n- The [API reference](/docs/API/bar) has a page per chart and one for the [tooltip](/docs/API/tooltip).\\n- The [Storybook](pathname:///storybook/) shows every chart, in core and in React, with its options live \u2014 that is usually faster than reading accessor names off a page.\\n\\n## Tell us what breaks\\n\\nA beta is only useful if people actually try to break it, so please do. Open an [issue](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/issues) with the chart, the data and what you expected instead \u2014 the more specific, the faster I can act on it.\\n\\nI will also say plainly what the [contributing guide](https://github.com/britecharts/britecharts/blob/main/.github/CONTRIBUTING.md) says: Britecharts needs maintainers. I have carried this release mostly on my own, and a 3.7k-star library with one person behind it is not a sustainable place to leave it. If any part of this codebase is something you would enjoy owning, that guide is where to start the conversation."}]}}')}}]);